WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.116
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level three.

2
00:00:06.416 --> 00:00:09.872
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.172 --> 00:00:17.504
Unit fifteen: Corruption & Accountability. Today you will use the fact that and the claim that to discuss wrongdoing.

4
00:00:17.804 --> 00:00:20.630
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.567 --> 00:00:24.939
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.783 --> 00:00:26.657
Bribery.

7
00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:30.996
Bribery means giving or taking money to influence a decision.

8
00:00:31.583 --> 00:00:34.094
Bribery destroyed the company's reputation.

9
00:00:34.683 --> 00:00:37.324
Your turn. Say: Bribery.

10
00:00:40.167 --> 00:00:41.040
Bribery.

11
00:00:41.783 --> 00:00:43.121
Conflict of interest.

12
00:00:44.117 --> 00:00:48.728
Conflict of interest means a situation in which personal gain affects official duty.

13
00:00:49.317 --> 00:00:52.361
The judge declared a conflict of interest and stepped down.

14
00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:56.125
Your turn. Say: Conflict of interest.

15
00:00:58.967 --> 00:01:00.815
Conflict of interest.

16
00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:02.876
Transparency.

17
00:01:03.883 --> 00:01:07.822
Transparency means openness that lets the public see how decisions are made.

18
00:01:08.417 --> 00:01:11.287
Transparency in contracts reduces corruption.

19
00:01:11.883 --> 00:01:14.861
Your turn. Say: Transparency.

20
00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:18.945
Transparency.

21
00:01:19.683 --> 00:01:21.288
Anti-corruption law.

22
00:01:22.283 --> 00:01:26.396
Anti-corruption law means a law designed to prevent and punish corruption.

23
00:01:26.983 --> 00:01:30.469
The anti-corruption law created an independent agency.

24
00:01:31.083 --> 00:01:33.945
Your turn. Say: Anti-corruption law.

25
00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:38.335
Anti-corruption law.

26
00:01:39.083 --> 00:01:40.700
Whistleblower protection.

27
00:01:41.683 --> 00:01:46.028
Whistleblower protection means legal safety for people who report wrongdoing.

28
00:01:46.617 --> 00:01:49.847
Whistleblower protection shielded the engineer from dismissal.

29
00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:53.915
Your turn. Say: Whistleblower protection.

30
00:01:56.767 --> 00:01:58.441
Whistleblower protection.

31
00:01:59.183 --> 00:02:00.347
Public trust.

32
00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:05.010
Public trust means the confidence citizens have in institutions.

33
00:02:05.617 --> 00:02:07.674
Public trust fell after the scandal.

34
00:02:08.283 --> 00:02:10.912
Your turn. Say: Public trust.

35
00:02:13.767 --> 00:02:15.081
Public trust.

36
00:02:15.817 --> 00:02:16.655
Audit.

37
00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:20.973
Audit means an official check of accounts or activities.

38
00:02:21.583 --> 00:02:23.711
An audit found missing funds.

39
00:02:24.317 --> 00:02:26.563
Your turn. Say: Audit.

40
00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:30.157
Audit.

41
00:02:30.917 --> 00:02:32.603
Accountability mechanism.

42
00:02:33.617 --> 00:02:38.275
Accountability mechanism means a system that makes officials answer for their actions.

43
00:02:38.883 --> 00:02:42.311
A parliamentary committee is an accountability mechanism.

44
00:02:42.917 --> 00:02:46.730
Your turn. Say: Accountability mechanism.

45
00:02:49.567 --> 00:02:51.647
Accountability mechanism.

46
00:02:52.433 --> 00:02:55.875
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:56.717 --> 00:03:02.794
Visal, an editorial says 'The fact that officials accepted bribes shocked the public.' Why add 'the fact'?

48
00:03:03.883 --> 00:03:18.494
Because a that-clause can be the subject but sounds heavy. 'The fact that', 'the idea that' and 'the claim that' make the clause a noun phrase, so it is easier to use after prepositions: despite the fact that.

49
00:03:19.583 --> 00:03:21.641
Which nouns allow that-clauses?

50
00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:37.779
Nouns of belief and information: fact, idea, claim, belief, evidence, report. In 'the report that the audit was hidden', the clause explains the noun and tells us what the report said.

51
00:03:38.883 --> 00:03:41.150
Give me a transparency example.

52
00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:54.272
The fact that contracts are public improves public trust. Despite the fact that anti-corruption laws exist, a weak accountability mechanism lets conflict of interest continue.

53
00:03:55.433 --> 00:03:56.968
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:57.817 --> 00:04:02.510
Grammar focus: Noun plus that-clause: the fact and claim that.

55
00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:04.055
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:07.945
The fact that officials accepted bribes shocked the public.

57
00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:11.802
The claim that the audit was hidden damaged public trust.

58
00:04:12.717 --> 00:04:16.783
Despite the fact that anti-corruption laws exist, abuse continues.

59
00:04:17.733 --> 00:04:26.806
Noun + that-clause: the fact / idea / claim / evidence that + subject + verb. After despite or in view of, use 'the fact that' with a full clause.

60
00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:31.886
Be careful! Don't say: Despite that the law exists, it fails.

61
00:04:33.833 --> 00:04:37.865
Say: Despite the fact that the law exists, it fails.

62
00:04:38.165 --> 00:04:40.245
Use 'the fact that' after despite.

63
00:04:41.033 --> 00:04:43.709
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:46.151
Trust and Accountability.

65
00:04:46.451 --> 00:04:50.053
Corruption harms a country far more than the money that disappears.

66
00:04:51.067 --> 00:04:57.942
The fact that a minister took a bribe damages public trust, and the evidence that a conflict of interest was hidden makes it worse.

67
00:04:58.933 --> 00:05:04.578
Transparency, such as publishing contracts, helps citizens check whether officials are honest.

68
00:05:05.567 --> 00:05:11.258
An independent audit and a clear accountability mechanism show that rules apply to powerful people too.

69
00:05:12.267 --> 00:05:17.819
Whistleblower protection matters because those who report wrongdoing are often the first to suffer.

70
00:05:18.933 --> 00:05:22.761
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:23.617 --> 00:05:26.603
blank officials took bribes shocked everyone.

72
00:05:26.903 --> 00:05:30.696
A, The fact that. B, The fact. C, That fact of.

73
00:05:34.233 --> 00:05:36.488
The answer is A: The fact that.

74
00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:40.474
Taking money to influence a decision is blank.

75
00:05:40.774 --> 00:05:44.636
A, an audit. B, bribery. C, transparency.

76
00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:50.083
The answer is B: bribery.

77
00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:53.726
An official check of accounts is an blank.

78
00:05:54.026 --> 00:05:57.319
A, appeal. B, apathy. C, audit.

79
00:06:00.867 --> 00:06:02.971
The answer is C: audit.

80
00:06:04.117 --> 00:06:05.872
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:06:06.172 --> 00:06:17.152
A, Despite the fact the law exists, abuse continues.. B, Despite the fact that the law exists, abuse continues.. C, Despite that the law exists, abuse continues..

82
00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:25.451
The answer is B: Despite the fact that the law exists, abuse continues..

83
00:06:26.617 --> 00:06:29.940
According to the reading, whistleblowers are often the first to blank.

84
00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:33.626
A, suffer. B, retire. C, vote.

85
00:06:37.167 --> 00:06:39.224
The answer is A: suffer.

86
00:06:40.433 --> 00:06:43.538
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:44.383 --> 00:06:48.322
Why is whistleblower protection important for exposing government corruption?

88
00:06:57.117 --> 00:06:58.721
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:59.021 --> 00:07:27.616
Whistleblowers often work inside an organisation, so they see wrongdoing that outsiders cannot. Without legal protection, they risk losing their jobs, facing threats or even prison. Strong whistleblower protection encourages people to report corruption safely, which helps journalists and investigators uncover abuse earlier. It therefore supports accountability and public trust in government.

90
00:07:27.916 --> 00:07:29.521
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:30.417 --> 00:07:36.006
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and noun plus that-clause: the fact and claim that.

92
00:07:36.306 --> 00:07:41.315
Today's value is integrity. Do the right thing, even when no one is watching.

93
00:07:41.615 --> 00:07:44.407
See you in Unit sixteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:44.707 --> 00:07:47.359
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

